<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 31 0 R/A[703 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(A growing retailer must keep feeding the business before it can enjoy the proceeds. Suppliers need paying, taxes come due and working capital shifts with the seasons. )/Pg 31 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(At Inditex, a negative  803 million working-capital movement in FY2025, against  198 million a year earlier, helps explain the subdued cash result. Fast Retailing's decline is clear too, though the accounts do not pin it on one culprit.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(THE BILL BEHIND THE BRIGHT LIGHTS)/Pg 31 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(Then there is the price of the next chapter. Fast Retailing's core capital spending jumped from ¥104.0 billion to ¥162.9 billion, or from 3.4% to 4.8% of revenue. Inditex spent  2.71 billion, around 6.8% of sales and close to the previous year's level. One was stepping up investment; the other was sustaining an already substantial programme. )/Pg 31 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 134 0 R/Pg 32 0 R/A[704 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 134 0 R/Pg 32 0 R/A[705 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 134 0 R/Pg 32 0 R/A[706 0 R 0]/K[2]>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(The ribbon is cut in a day. The lease can last for years. Under IFRS 16, most store leases appear as right-of-use assets and matching liabilities. At FY2025 year-end, those liabilities stood at ¥513.5 billion for Fast Retailing and  5.93 billion for Inditex. They are discounted future obligations, not the rent paid that year„and the currencies are not a useful basis for ranking their size.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(So what is left after the business pays for core investment and the financing lease cash flows it separately reports? On this deliberately narrower test, Fast Retailing retained about ¥277.3 billion, or 8.2% of revenue; Inditex retained  4.69 billion, or 11.8%. We call it post-lease cash, not IFRS free cash flow. Lease-payment labels differ between the groups, so the comparison is illuminating rather than perfectly like-for-like.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(A STORE OPENS. THE RENT STAYS.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(Inditex kept a larger share of FY2025 sales after these selected cash outlays, despite slower revenue growth. That is a real advantage on this measure, not a prophecy. Fast Retailing's heavier investment could pay off handsomely„or fail to earn its keep. One year's cash balance cannot settle that question.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(A new flagship makes a splendid photograph. The more consequential picture arrives years later: customers returning, investment earning its return, rent still falling due. In fashion, opening the doors is the easy part of the growth story.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(THE LEDGER'S TAKE)/Pg 32 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 135 0 R/Pg 33 0 R/A[707 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(After the financial statements, the story returns to the shop floor)/Pg 33 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(THE RACE ISN'T WHAT IT SEEMS)/Pg 33 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(Scale versus speed sounds like a neat explanation for two fashion giants. The financial statements make the story less tidy.)/Pg 33 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(Over those five years, Inditex consistently turned inventory faster and reported higher gross and operating margins than Fast Retailing. In FY2025 it also retained a larger share of revenue after selected capital investment and lease payments.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(That is a convincing financial lead„not proof that Zara's speed caused it. These are group accounts, covering more than the two flagship brands. Pricing, product mix, geography and accounting presentation all complicate the explanation.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(Meanwhile, Fast Retailing has narrowed part of the inventory-efficiency gap since FY2022. Its operating margin improved in FY2025 even as gross margin edged lower. The financial gap is clear. The reasons behind it are less easily captured in a spreadsheet.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(THE SCOREBOARD IS NOT THE STORY)/Pg 34 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj
397 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(The brands still sell different promises. Yet both need accurate demand signals, coordinated stock and stores worth investing in.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj
398 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(The questions they face are surprisingly similar. Even though annual reports tell us what the businesses achieved, they cannot show every choice a customer sees. )/Pg 34 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj
399 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(To see what the accounts leave out, we headed to Singapore's Orchard Road, where UNIQLO and Zara compete for shoppers just a short distance apart.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj
